@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: how the choice prompt stays usable on a terminal shorter than the form
' covers: internal/choiceui/viewport.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/canvas.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/layout.go
'   the budget it extends ....... _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-strip.puml
'   the figure in the foot ...... _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-states.puml
'   the resolved layout ......... _spec/internal/choiceui/wireframe.puml
'   what is still unbuilt ....... _spec/_plans/choiceui-viewport-proposals.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
title Choice-prompt viewport — a droppable head, a scrolling body, a foot that follows

node "internal/choiceui" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "bodyLines()\nevery painted line, and its row" as LINES <<app>>
  component "scrollTo(prev, window, lines, cursor)\nthe offset that keeps the cursor on" as SCROLL <<app>>
  component "gutter\nends of travel · proportional thumb" as GUTTER <<app>>
  component "canvas\none writer, one window, drops outside" as CANVAS <<app>>
  component "layout(w, h)\nthe ladder, now yielding a body height" as LAYOUT <<app>>
}

component "head — banner · title · header · axis\ndroppable, never scrolled" as HEAD <<app>>
component "body — the rows\nthe only region that scrolls" as BODY <<app>>
component "foot — hint · help slot · strip\nfollows the body, never painted over" as FOOT <<app>>
component "Operator terminal\nw x h, resized at will" as TTY <<host>>
component "A form that loses its last rows in silence" as LOST <<error>>

LINES ARROW_MAIN SCROLL
LINES ARROW_MAIN LAYOUT : its length IS rowsHeight
SCROLL ARROW_MAIN GUTTER
LAYOUT ARROW_MAIN BODY : how many lines it may paint
CANVAS ARROW_MAIN HEAD
CANVAS ARROW_MAIN BODY
CANVAS ARROW_MAIN FOOT
HEAD ARROW_MAIN TTY
BODY ARROW_MAIN TTY
FOOT ARROW_MAIN TTY
LOST ARROW_ERROR FOOT : what this replaces

note top of LINES
  **The body scrolls by LINE, never by row.** A
  divider costs four lines and a plain row one,
  so an offset counted in rows would travel four
  at a time and could never put a heading and
  its own row on screen together.
end note

note bottom of LINES
  **One description of the height, not two.** The
  formula and the paint loop used to state it
  separately and could drift; the painter now
  walks the same list the budget measures, so
  the two cannot disagree.
end note

note top of SCROLL
  **The offset is a cache, never a source of
  truth.** Every paint re-clamps it and re-earns
  the margin, so a form drawn again at another
  size cannot inherit an offset that stopped
  making sense when the terminal changed.
end note

note bottom of SCROLL
  **The whole group comes on screen when it
  fits.** Checkboxes arriving without the heading
  that names them cannot be identified — and when
  the window is too small for the furniture, the
  row itself is what wins.
end note

note bottom of GUTTER
  **A divider row takes its label back.** The
  heading names the group, so the row is drawn
  bare — but the heading can scroll away, and a
  nameless group of boxes is worse than saying
  the name twice.
end note

note top of CANVAS
  **Outside the window a write is DROPPED, not
  clipped.** A half-painted row is worse than an
  absent one, and tcell would happily let a
  scrolled-off row paint over the hint — which is
  the silent failure being ended here.
end note

note bottom of CANVAS
  **It landed as a proven no-op.** With the window
  wide open it is the closure it replaces, so the
  whole suite passed before anything scrolled and
  the flip could be judged on its own.
end note

note top of LAYOUT
  **The body's FLOOR is priced up front.** Running
  the ladder, finding the body too small and
  dropping another region is a feedback loop that
  can oscillate — and it would break the
  monotonicity the strict ladder exists for.
end note

note bottom of LAYOUT
  **The foot FOLLOWS the body.** Pinned means
  always drawn, never scrolled off — not fixed to
  the bottom edge. So a form that fits renders
  exactly where it did before the viewport, with
  no offset and no gutter drawn at all.
end note

note bottom of FOOT
  **The count rides on the hint.** A region that
  exists to say the rows ran out must not be the
  reason one more of them did, so "N more below"
  costs no body line of its own.
end note

note bottom of LOST
  **Below the floor the failure is loud.** The
  prompt names the size it needs and draws
  nothing else, and the event loop keeps running:
  a terminal too small to draw the form must
  never be one the operator cannot leave.
end note

note bottom of TTY
  **No new minimum width.** The gutter costs two
  columns, the options run to about seventy, and
  a row's inline reason was already clipped — so
  the strip's own 72 stays the only width floor
  the prompt has.
end note
@enduml
